WEBVTT

00:07.250 --> 00:09.922
Our breathing says a lot about our health.

00:10.350 --> 00:12.760
Coughs, sneezes and sniffs,

00:12.800 --> 00:16.986
and whether we breathe through the
nose or the mouth, deeply or shallowly,

00:17.026 --> 00:20.545
can all reflect the state
of our respiratory system.

00:21.550 --> 00:26.212
Following these behaviors throughout the
day could support self-monitoring and care.

00:26.252 --> 00:29.609
Yet today's options come with trade-offs.

00:30.250 --> 00:33.519
Chest belts and masks are
accurate, but obtrusive.

00:33.559 --> 00:36.712
Earbuds can become
uncomfortable over long periods,

00:36.752 --> 00:40.445
and their microphones also
pick up the sounds around us.

00:41.750 --> 00:43.788
We present RespiraFrame,

00:43.828 --> 00:49.066
an eyeglass frame that senses respiration
through two complementary channels.

00:50.150 --> 00:55.393
The first is a bone-conduction microphone,
embedded in the frame beside the nose.

00:55.433 --> 01:00.438
It captures breathing-related vibrations
as they travel through the face.

01:01.150 --> 01:06.655
Unlike a conventional microphone, it is
only weakly affected by airborne noise.

01:07.950 --> 01:11.114
The second is electrical impedance tomography.

01:11.154 --> 01:15.677
Eight soft dry electrodes sit where
glasses already touch the skin:

01:15.717 --> 01:18.192
on the nose pads and the temples.

01:18.950 --> 01:22.811
A small, safe current is injected
between neighboring electrodes,

01:22.851 --> 01:25.145
yielding 64 measurements per cycle

01:25.185 --> 01:29.052
that follow how facial
tissue changes as we breathe.

01:29.950 --> 01:34.380
To design the frame, we compared four
placements for the bone-conduction sensor,

01:34.420 --> 01:37.823
and chose the one closest to the nasal airway.

01:38.650 --> 01:43.962
We also compared three mounting structures,
and embedded the sensor inside the frame,

01:44.002 --> 01:47.824
so that the frame itself
shields it from airborne noise.

01:49.250 --> 01:52.240
Every four seconds, both
signals are preprocessed

01:52.280 --> 01:54.690
and encoded by a two-branch network,

01:54.730 --> 01:58.424
whose features are fused
with multi-head attention.

01:59.350 --> 02:03.942
RespiraFrame recognizes five
abnormal respiratory events:

02:03.982 --> 02:08.691
coughing, sneezing, sniffing,
nose blowing and throat clearing.

02:08.731 --> 02:12.035
It also distinguishes nasal from oral breathing,

02:12.075 --> 02:16.233
deep from shallow breathing,
and breathing from breath holding.

02:17.050 --> 02:19.587
In a lab study with 19 participants,

02:19.627 --> 02:24.963
RespiraFrame reached 92.5%
accuracy for abnormal events,

02:25.003 --> 02:30.775
and 84.8 to 88.0% for the three breathing tasks.

02:31.650 --> 02:34.408
With background noise of up to 80 decibels,

02:34.448 --> 02:38.455
accuracy changed by less
than 3 percentage points.

02:39.350 --> 02:41.969
When a person nearby coughed or sneezed,

02:42.009 --> 02:46.759
only 2.7% of these events
were attributed to the wearer.

02:47.650 --> 02:54.170
Nasal and oral breathing stayed above
93% while walking or running in place,

02:54.210 --> 02:59.749
whereas abnormal events were more affected
by intense motion and strong wind.

03:00.850 --> 03:05.837
Ten participants then wore RespiraFrame
in an office, a cafeteria and outdoors,

03:05.877 --> 03:07.764
for 30 hours in total.

03:07.804 --> 03:12.485
It reached 87.6% for abnormal events.

03:13.850 --> 03:18.751
In a four-week follow-up, its
accuracy showed no systematic decline.

03:19.550 --> 03:23.701
Participants rated the glasses as
comfortable and socially acceptable,

03:23.741 --> 03:30.395
and as a more suitable form factor for everyday
respiratory monitoring than masks or earbuds.

03:31.950 --> 03:34.947
RespiraFrame also runs in real time.

03:35.300 --> 03:37.798
Here, it recognizes throat clearing,

03:40.387 --> 03:41.318
a sniff,

03:42.248 --> 03:43.515
and a sneeze,

03:45.352 --> 03:49.231
while a bystander's actions are
not attributed to the wearer.

03:50.399 --> 03:53.849
It follows changes between
nasal and oral breathing,

04:03.104 --> 04:06.101
even with a hair dryer blowing nearby.

04:12.718 --> 04:14.508
It detects breath holding,

04:24.508 --> 04:27.366
and tells deep from shallow breathing.

04:32.150 --> 04:35.999
RespiraFrame suggests that the
glasses many of us already wear

04:36.039 --> 04:40.534
could become a comfortable, everyday
platform for respiratory monitoring.
